  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 242 PM CDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nRecent water vapor imagery showing upper ridging slowly building \nnorth into the central High Plains.  Local winds beginning to show \nsome response to the modest surface axis nudging east over eastern \nKansas with easterly winds in much of the area. Much of the area has \nseen clearing but some mid and high cloud and event an isolated \nshower has kept more cloud in place in western areas.\n\nThe pattern slowly changes over the next several days with the upper \nridge building on northeast into the Missouri Valley with the \nsurface ridge nudging east. Moisture levels aloft will slowly \nincrease, with some minor isentropic lift noted around 700 mb in \ncentral Kansas late tonight into Wednesday morning coinciding with \nthe beginning of the moisture increase. Instability remains very \nweak but the lift could be enough for some weak showers and sided \nwith some of the latest CAMs over NBM for some precip mention. There \nis minor concern for fog development late tonight into early \nWednesday with modest winds under little cloud tonight, though most \nguidance develops stratus from northeast to southwest again tonight. \nWinds of 10-15 mph in the boundary layer should help mitigate any \nfog, and have left it out of the forecast.\n\nPrecipitation chances increase more notably Wednesday night into \nFriday as moisture/moisture advection/weak isentropic lift increase \nacross the area. PW values still look to reach around 1.7 inches but \nlimited instability and the lack of any focusing mechanism should \nkeep heavy rainfall potential low. NBM and EPS showing the 90th \npercentile for precipitation over 12 hours at an inch or less. \nLimited mid-level lapse rates and considerable cloud along with \nmodest winds aloft should keep severe weather in check. Confidence \nin high temperatures for these periods remains somewhat diminished \nbased on precipitation coverage and persistence questions though \nrecent trends bring a shortwave through the northern Plains Saturday \nto provide drying and warming conditions. \n\nA more progressive zonal flow regime sets up early next week with a \nanother wave and likely cold front Monday night into Tuesday. At \nthis point, severe thunderstorm potential looks low with shear again \nlimited.",
